    "q-143509": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143509",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n=30$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.03,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-3.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ |\n| $\\,-3.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ |\n| $\\,-3.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0012\\,$ | $\\,0.0011\\,$ | $\\,0.0011\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =0.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.03.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0.8,\\frac{0.03}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.8,0.001).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.7-0.8}{\\sqrt{0.001}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-3.16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>-3.16) & =1-P(Z<-3.16) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-3.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nΦ(-3.16)=0.0008.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =1-Φ(-3.16) \\\\  & =1-0.0008 \\\\  & =0.9992.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
