    "q-143510": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143510",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n=30$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.2).$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2389\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ | $\\,0.2327\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2709\\,$ | $\\,0.2676\\,$ | $\\,0.2643\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.3050\\,$ | $\\,0.3015\\,$ | $\\,0.2981\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=30,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 0.3+(-1)\\cdot 0.2+0\\cdot 0.1+2\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =-0.6-0.2+0+0.8 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =((-2)^{2}\\cdot 0.3+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{0}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.4)-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(1.2+0.2+0+1.6)-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0,\\frac{3}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(0,0.1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.2) & =P(\\overset{―}{X}<-0.2)+P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.2) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<\\frac{-0.2-0}{\\sqrt{0.1}})+P(Z>\\frac{0.2-0}{\\sqrt{0.1}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-0.63)+P(Z>0.63) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.63)+1-P(Z<0.63) \\\\  & =1+Φ(-0.63)-Φ(0.63).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.63)=0.2643.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, by the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\,Φ(0.63)=1-Φ(-0.63).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.2) & =1+Φ(-0.63)-Φ(0.63) \\\\  & =2Φ(-0.63) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.2643 \\\\  & =0.5286.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
